define-telegram-commands-in-app-server

Lane: cross

Unit: send-notifications-through-telegram

ChildPatch: define-telegram-commands-in-app-server

Form: GitDiff

Segment: schema

Purpose: Configure independently selectable Telegram notifications for successful primary turns, live input and approvals, Codex prereleases, Codex releases and model changes.

Requires: Unit:cross/host-dialogs-with-shared-resources, Unit:cross/analyze-model-changes-after-synchronization, Unit:cross/configure-integrations-through-shared-file, Unit:cross/configure-application-proxy-at-runtime, Unit:cross/monitor-codex-releases-through-github

Provides: Expose typed telegram/command settings and delivery, bot-only setup validation before shared-file persistence, credential-redacted bounded delivery through shared routing, five independent notification selections, native masked setup and multiselect dialogs, and subscriptions to the model-change and GitHub producers. Preserve successful-delivery deduplication, completion duration filtering, source-session title attribution, separate silent release baselines and retry eligibility after failed release delivery.

Boundary: Shared configuration owns integrations.json persistence; GitHub owns release queries, cadence and observations; model analysis owns change publication; the Control Center owns settings hosting and shortcuts. Telegram owns no GitHub credentials, query settings, legacy file reader, runtime status or sent counter. Preserve native terminal notifications and approvals, exclude historical questions, failed or interrupted turns, secondary-thread completions, legacy input tools and plan prompts, and cancel background delivery on shutdown. Setting reads do not wait for network commands; selection changes do not notify historical releases or consume another channel delivery baseline.

Verification: Protocol field-shape, selection and redaction tests; bounded HTTP and setup persistence tests; selected delivery, baseline, failure and cancellation tests; real RPC queue independence and shared-file integration; multiselect setup and embedded dialog refresh; primary completion, live question and approval consumers with source identity, title changes, buffering and exclusion coverage.

CrossGroup: control-center


